"""Original plasma-cell chapter: sixteen diagnostic and myeloma-care questions."""
from qbank_authoring import question,ref,finalize
IM=ref('IMWG: Diagnostic Criteria for Multiple Myeloma','https://www.myeloma.org/international-myeloma-working-group-imwg-criteria-diagnosis-multiple-myeloma')
MY=ref('NCI: Plasma Cell Neoplasms—Health Professional PDQ','https://www.cancer.gov/types/myeloma/hp/myeloma-treatment-pdq')
SM=ref('FDA: Daratumumab/Hyaluronidase for High-Risk Smoldering Myeloma, 2025','https://www.fda.gov/drugs/resources-information-approved-drugs/fda-approves-daratumumab-and-hyaluronidase-fihj-high-risk-smoldering-multiple-myeloma')
rows=[]
def q(*a,**k):rows.append(question(*a,**k))
q('An adult has an IgG M protein 1.2 g/dL, marrow clonal plasma cells 5%, no attributable organ injury and no myeloma-defining biomarker. Which category best fits?', 'Non-IgM MGUS',[
 ('Smoldering myeloma','The marrow and M-protein burden are below the usual thresholds in this scenario.'),('Active multiple myeloma','No myeloma-defining event is described.'),('Solitary plasmacytoma','No localized biopsy-proven plasma-cell mass is described.')],
 'A monoclonal protein is a finding that requires classification', '**MGUS has a limited clonal burden without a defining injury or event.**',['Assess whether any organ abnormality is actually attributable to the clone.','Some small clones cause clinically important monoclonal-protein disease despite not meeting myeloma criteria.'], '**Use risk-based surveillance and clear return advice.**',['Follow the protein, counts and relevant organ measures.','New pain, renal change or anemia requires reassessment.'],[['Marrow','Less than 10% in this case'],['M protein','Below 3 g/dL'],['Defining injury / event','Absent']],[IM,MY],style='clinical_scenario')
q('An adult has marrow clonal plasma cells 20%, an M protein 2.2 g/dL and no CRAB injury or SLiM event. Which category best fits?', 'Smoldering multiple myeloma',[
 ('MGUS','The marrow clonal percentage exceeds the MGUS range.'),('Active myeloma solely because the marrow exceeds 10%','A myeloma-defining event is still needed.'),('Solitary plasmacytoma without marrow disease','There is a systemic marrow clone.')],
 'Clonal burden and defining events answer different questions', '**A 10% to less-than-60% marrow burden can be smoldering when defining events are absent.**',['The full protein and clinical criteria must be assessed.','Risk varies considerably within smoldering disease.'], '**Risk-stratify and discuss the current management options.**',['Observation remains appropriate in many patients.','Selected high-risk disease now has evidence-based treatment options in defined settings.'],[['MGUS','Lower marrow burden'],['Smoldering','Increased burden without defining event'],['Active myeloma','Requires a defining event']],[IM,MY],style='clinical_scenario')
q('Which marrow finding is an IMWG myeloma-defining biomarker in the appropriate clonal plasma-cell setting?', 'At least 60% clonal plasma cells',[
 ('At least 5% polyclonal plasma cells','This does not meet the defining clonal threshold.'),('At least 10% clonal plasma cells with no other event in every patient','That alone can be smoldering rather than active disease.'),('Any isolated plasma cell on a smear','This is not a defining biomarker.')],
 'SLiM criteria identify high progression risk before classic injury', '**A clonal marrow percentage at least 60 is one defining event.**',['The other biomarkers involve the FLC ratio and MRI focal lesions.','Use an adequate representative marrow assessment.'], '**Complete staging and treatment-readiness evaluation.**',['Do not wait for preventable renal or skeletal injury once active disease criteria are established.','Account for sampling and laboratory uncertainty when a result is discordant.'],[['S','At least 60% clonal marrow plasma cells'],['Li','Qualifying FLC ratio'],['M','Qualifying MRI focal lesions']],[IM])
q('Which free-light-chain result meets the IMWG SLiM biomarker criterion?', 'Involved/uninvolved ratio at least 100 with involved FLC at least 100 mg/L',[
 ('Ratio 100 with involved FLC 8 mg/L','The absolute involved-chain requirement is not met.'),('Ratio 10 with involved FLC 200 mg/L','The ratio requirement is not met.'),('Both chains mildly elevated with a ratio in the appropriate reference range','This can reflect impaired renal clearance and does not meet the criterion.')],
 'The ratio and absolute involved-chain concentration must both qualify', '**A ratio alone is not the complete criterion.**',['Know which chain is involved.','Check units: 100 mg/L equals 10 mg/dL.'], '**Interpret the assay within the full diagnostic setting.**',['Renal function can affect concentrations and reference interpretation.','Do not assign active myeloma from a mildly abnormal ratio alone.'],[['Ratio','At least 100'],['Involved concentration','At least 100 mg/L'],['Requirement','Both with the appropriate clonal setting']],[IM])
q('Which MRI finding meets the IMWG focal-lesion biomarker criterion?', 'More than one focal lesion, each at least 5 mm',[
 ('One 3-mm nonspecific focus','This does not meet the criterion.'),('Diffuse degenerative change without focal marrow lesions','This is not the specified biomarker.'),('Any osteoporosis on plain radiography','Osteoporosis alone is not this myeloma-defining MRI event.')],
 'The MRI criterion is numerical and size-specific', '**At least two qualifying focal lesions are required.**',['Each must be at least 5 mm.','Equivocal findings need an appropriate radiological follow-up strategy.'], '**Distinguish marrow lesions from unrelated degenerative findings.**',['Use the complete imaging and clinical context.','A CT-defined lytic lesion belongs to the bone-injury pathway rather than this exact MRI biomarker.'],[['Number','More than one'],['Size','At least 5 mm each'],['Interpretation','Qualifying focal marrow lesions']],[IM])
q('A patient has an M protein, chronic anemia from a clearly documented unrelated cause and marrow plasma cells 8%. What is the correct CRAB principle?', 'Organ injury must be attributable to the plasma-cell disorder rather than merely coexist with it',[
 ('Any anemia plus any M protein proves active myeloma','Coincidental MGUS and another anemia can coexist.'),('An M protein excludes every alternative cause of renal or blood-count abnormalities','It does not.'),('Only the protein concentration matters and organ injury is irrelevant','Both clonal burden and defining events matter.')],
 'Attribution prevents overdiagnosis and missed disease', '**CRAB findings are not a checklist of unrelated comorbidities.**',['Evaluate the mechanism of anemia, renal impairment and bone disease.','Small clones can nevertheless cause specific organ disease such as MGRS or AL.'], '**Classify the actual clone-related syndrome.**',['A patient can need clone-directed treatment without meeting conventional myeloma criteria in selected organ disorders.','Do not infer treatment from the presence of a spike alone.'],[['Coincidence','Common in older adults'],['Attribution','Required for CRAB injury'],['Other clonal disease','May have a separate organ-based indication']],[IM,MY],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A patient has lytic lesions and suspected plasma-cell disease, but serum protein electrophoresis shows no clear M spike. What is the best next principle?', 'Assess serum FLC, immunofixation, urine studies and marrow as appropriate because light-chain or low-secretory disease remains possible',[
 ('Exclude myeloma permanently','SPEP can miss clinically important light-chain disease.'),('Diagnose osteoporosis without further evaluation','The lytic pattern requires an explanation.'),('Use total serum protein alone as a definitive exclusion','Total protein can be unremarkable in light-chain disease.')],
 'The secreted protein pattern determines test sensitivity', '**SPEP alone is not a complete monoclonal-protein evaluation.**',['Immunofixation is more sensitive for small monoclonal components.','FLC testing captures an important light-chain phenotype.'], '**Use tissue and imaging to resolve the diagnosis.**',['Renal and skeletal injury may be substantial despite a small or absent intact-immunoglobulin spike.','True nonsecretory disease requires careful characterization.'],[['SPEP','May miss light-chain disease'],['Additional tests','FLC and immunofixation with relevant urine studies'],['Confirmation','Marrow and imaging context']],[IM,MY],style='clinical_scenario')
q('Why should serum free-light-chain concentrations be interpreted cautiously in CKD?', 'Reduced renal clearance can elevate both chains and alter the appropriate ratio reference interpretation',[
 ('CKD makes every abnormal ratio definitely monoclonal','A renal effect can be nonclonal.'),('Normalizing the ratio by guesswork establishes the diagnosis','Use validated assay and renal-context interpretation.'),('FLC testing has no value in any patient with kidney disease','It remains important when interpreted correctly.')],
 'Concentration reflects production and clearance', '**Renal impairment can increase circulating FLC levels.**',['The ratio and involved-chain pattern help assess clonality.','Reference intervals can be assay- and kidney-function-dependent.'], '**Use immunofixation and clinical evidence when uncertainty remains.**',['Do not miss a light-chain clone by dismissing all abnormalities as renal.','Do not overdiagnose a clone from mild symmetrical elevations.'],[['Production','Clonal or polyclonal'],['Clearance','Reduced in CKD'],['Interpretation','Assay and renal context']],[IM,MY])
q('Why is a routine radionuclide bone scan less suitable than appropriate CT/MRI/PET-based assessment for typical myeloma skeletal disease?', 'Predominantly osteolytic lesions may have little osteoblastic activity and be underestimated',[
 ('Myeloma lesions always produce intense osteoblastic uptake','That is not the usual lesion biology.'),('CT cannot detect cortical destruction','CT is useful for lytic bone assessment.'),('MRI has no role in marrow or cord evaluation','It is important in appropriate settings.')],
 'Choose imaging according to the lesion biology', '**Myeloma frequently causes destructive osteolytic disease.**',['Low-dose whole-body CT is useful for bone destruction.','MRI is valuable for marrow and neurological complications.'], '**Apply diagnostic imaging thresholds correctly.**',['PET uptake alone without the required underlying lytic change does not automatically meet the CRAB bone criterion.','Use the relevant imaging framework and expert interpretation.'],[['Bone scan','Depends on osteoblastic response'],['CT','Cortical/lytic destruction'],['MRI','Marrow and neural structures']],[IM,MY])
q('A myeloma patient develops severe back pain, new leg weakness and urinary retention. What is the priority?', 'Urgent assessment for spinal cord compression with appropriate MRI and immediate specialist treatment planning',[
 ('Routine outpatient imaging in three months','Neurological loss can become irreversible.'),('Assume this is uncomplicated chronic bone pain','The neurological and sphincter findings are red flags.'),('Treat only with oral iron','This does not address the emergency.')],
 'A skeletal complication can become a neurological emergency', '**New weakness or sphincter dysfunction requires urgent action.**',['Assess stability and the level of compression.','Corticosteroids and local/systemic treatment follow the emergency oncology plan.'], '**Coordinate hematology, spinal surgery and radiation expertise as appropriate.**',['Mechanical instability can change the intervention.','Do not wait for a routine clinic visit to reassess.'],[['Red flags','Weakness and sphincter change'],['Imaging','Urgent MRI pathway'],['Treatment','Immediate multidisciplinary plan']],[MY],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A patient with newly diagnosed light-chain myeloma develops rapidly worsening AKI and a high involved FLC burden. What is the key treatment principle?', 'Prompt clone-directed therapy and correction of reversible renal insults, with urgent specialist assessment for cast nephropathy',[
 ('Wait for months of observation because renal injury is always irreversible','Early effective treatment can preserve renal recovery potential.'),('Use NSAIDs freely for pain despite AKI','Nephrotoxic exposures can worsen injury.'),('Routine high-cutoff dialysis alone replaces antimyeloma therapy','Extracorporeal light-chain removal does not replace rapid control of production.')],
 'Renal recovery depends on stopping the pathogenic light-chain supply', '**Cast nephropathy is an important urgent mechanism.**',['Assess hydration, hypercalcemia, infection and nephrotoxic drugs.','Atypical renal findings may require biopsy to identify another mechanism.'], '**Use an effective renal-appropriate antimyeloma regimen.**',['Bortezomib-based therapy and dexamethasone are important components in suitable cases.','Dialysis is provided for standard indications; specialized removal strategies have evidence limitations.'],[['Immediate goal','Rapid clone control'],['Renal support','Correct contributors and provide indicated dialysis'],['Differential','Cast, AL, deposition or unrelated disease']],[MY],style='clinical_scenario')
q('What changed in the US management landscape for high-risk smoldering myeloma in November 2025?', 'Daratumumab/hyaluronidase received approval for adults with high-risk SMM',[
 ('Every MGUS patient became eligible for the same treatment','The indication is a defined high-risk SMM population.'),('All observation strategies were abolished','Risk and patient context still guide management.'),('The drug became an iron-chelation treatment','It is a CD38-directed therapy.')],
 'An older rule of observation for all smoldering disease is no longer complete', '**The approval is specific to high-risk smoldering myeloma.**',['Accurate risk definition and exclusion of already active myeloma are important.','Benefits and treatment burden require discussion.'], '**Do not generalize to all precursor states.**',['Many MGUS and lower-risk SMM patients remain under surveillance.','Use the current local label and specialist pathway.'],[['New option','Daratumumab/hyaluronidase'],['Population','Adult high-risk SMM'],['Limit','Not every monoclonal gammopathy']],[SM])
q('Which is a current frontline framework for many fit newly diagnosed myeloma patients?', 'A proteasome inhibitor, immunomodulatory drug and dexamethasone, often with an anti-CD38 antibody according to eligibility',[
 ('Melphalan alone as the mandatory regimen for every adult','Modern combinations and fitness-based strategies have changed care.'),('ATRA/arsenic for all plasma-cell disease','That is the APL pathway.'),('Anticoagulation alone as clone-directed therapy','It does not treat the plasma-cell clone.')],
 'Modern treatment combines complementary antimyeloma mechanisms', '**Exact induction depends on transplant eligibility, risk and comorbidity.**',['Anti-CD38 combinations are important options.','Frailty can require dose and regimen adaptation.'], '**Plan the entire sequence rather than only the first cycle.**',['Stem-cell collection, transplant and maintenance may be relevant.','Infection, bone and thrombosis prevention are part of care.'],[['Induction','Mechanism-based combination'],['Patient factors','Fitness, organs and risk'],['Sequence','Collection/transplant and maintenance when appropriate']],[MY])
q('What is the role of autologous stem-cell transplantation in eligible myeloma patients?', 'It permits high-dose treatment and can deepen disease control, but is not a guaranteed permanent cure',[
 ('It requires an HLA-matched sibling donor','Autologous transplantation uses the patient’s own cells.'),('It eliminates all future maintenance and monitoring needs','Ongoing treatment and follow-up are often required.'),('It is appropriate for every patient regardless of organ function and frailty','Eligibility is individualized.')],
 'Autologous and allogeneic transplantation have different purposes and risks', '**Autologous rescue supports recovery after high-dose therapy.**',['It does not provide the same donor immune effect as allogeneic transplantation.','Response durability varies by disease biology and subsequent care.'], '**Discuss toxicity, timing and long-term strategy.**',['Fertility, infections and recovery burden matter.','Use maintenance and follow-up according to current evidence.'],[['Cell source','Patient’s own cells'],['Purpose','Support high-dose therapy'],['Limit','Not universal permanent eradication']],[MY])
q('Which toxicity/prevention pair is particularly relevant during bortezomib-based myeloma treatment?', 'Peripheral neuropathy assessment and herpes-zoster prophylaxis',[
 ('Mandatory vitamin K reversal before every dose','This is not the standard mechanism-based precaution.'),('No infection precautions because it is targeted','Targeted therapy still alters risk.'),('Routine iron chelation in every nontransfused patient','That is not a universal requirement.')],
 'Proteasome inhibition has predictable supportive-care needs', '**Neuropathy can affect route, schedule and dose decisions.**',['Subcutaneous and schedule adaptations can improve tolerability in suitable regimens.','Herpes-zoster reactivation prevention is important.'], '**Monitor the whole treatment burden.**',['Counts, infection and autonomic symptoms can matter.','Do not continue a disabling toxicity unchanged without review.'],[['Neurology','Peripheral neuropathy'],['Infection prevention','Zoster prophylaxis'],['Adjustment','Dose/schedule according to toxicity']],[MY])
q('A patient receiving an IMiD-containing myeloma regimen needs thrombosis prevention. Which principle is correct?', 'Use an individualized VTE and bleeding-risk assessment to choose appropriate prophylaxis',[
 ('Every patient should receive aspirin regardless of prior VTE or bleeding','Higher-risk patients can require anticoagulant prophylaxis and bleeding can contraindicate it.'),('IMiDs eliminate VTE risk','They can increase risk, especially in combinations.'),('Prophylaxis is determined only by the M-protein value','Patient and regimen factors matter.')],
 'The regimen and the patient both contribute to thrombosis risk', '**Prior VTE, immobility, comorbidity and treatment intensity influence prevention.**',['Aspirin can be suitable in selected lower-risk settings.','Anticoagulant options are considered for greater risk under guidance.'], '**Reassess when the clinical state changes.**',['Renal function, platelets and procedures affect safety.','Teach symptoms of thrombosis and bleeding.'],[['Risk inputs','Patient and regimen'],['Choice','Risk-appropriate prophylaxis'],['Review','Bleeding, kidneys and counts']],[MY])
